uthor"><name><surname>Kronegg</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Voloshynovskiy</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Pun</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Analysis of bit-rate definitions for brain-computer interfaces</article-title><conf-name>In: Proceedings of the 11th International Conference on Human-Computer Interaction (HCI '05)</conf-name><conf-date>June 2005</conf-date><conf-loc>Las Vegas, Nevada, USA</conf-loc><fpage>40</fpage><lpage>46</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><label>25</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pfurtscheller</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Neuper</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Motor imagery direct communication</article-title><source><italic>Proceedings of the IEEE</italic></source><year>2001</year><volume>89</volume><issue>7</issue><fpage>1123</fpage><lpage>1134</lpage></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="fig1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>The stimulators placementand the graphic user interface layout.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CIN2009-864564.001"/></fig><fig id="fig2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Timings and structures of the adopted (a) screening and (b) training protocols.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CIN2009-864564.002"/></fig><fig id="fig3" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>An example of a joint time-frequency analysis (JTFA) calculated on ascreening dataset varying the stimulation frequency in the 6&#x02013;17 Hz range.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CIN2009-864564.003"/></fig><fig id="fig4" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Conceptual scheme of the processing and identification chain for each of thefour stimulation frequencies (<italic>s</italic>1, <italic>s</italic>2, <italic>s</italic>3, <italic>s</italic>4).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CIN2009-864564.004"/></fig><fig id="fig5" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>The adopted electrodes montage includes orange highlighted channels{T5,P3,Pz,P4,T6,O1,Oz,O2} using a linked mastoids reference and a groundchannel placed in GND position.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CIN2009-864564.005"/></fig><fig id="fig6" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Normalized weights associated to each electrode of the considered electrodesmontage calculated using the Common Spatial Patterns method. Most of thesubjects show a dominant activation localized over the O1-Oz-O2 electrodepositions and, according to the weight signs, the optimal filter is usually abipolar derivation possibly reinforced by parietotemporal activations.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CIN2009-864564.006"/></fig><fig id="fig7" position="float"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Weights associated to each electrode of the considered electrodes montageusing the manual channels combining approach described in <xref ref-type="sec" rid="subsubsec2.2.2">Section 2.2.2</xref>. Positiveweights refer to <italic>positive-contribution</italic> electrodes, while negativeweights refer to <italic>negative-contribution</italic> electrodes.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CIN2009-864564.007"/></fig><fig id="fig8" position="float"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>Average results across all subjects(G1 + G2)  obtained using the manual (blueline) and CSP-based (green line) methods with different lengths of the analysiswindow; (a) error rate, (b) delay time, and (c) bit-rate calculated using Wolpaw'sdefinition.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CIN2009-864564.008"/></fig><fig id="fig9" position="float"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>Occurrences of each stimulation frequency of the screened 6&#x02013;17 Hz range calculatedon both G1 and G2 control groups.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CIN2009-864564.009"/></fig><table-wrap id="